Final task · Design your own tree of life
The final task has two stages. First compare the same evolutionary event across two TimeTree files. Then use one of the eukaryote files to design and save your own tree-of-life visualisation.

1 · Compare the two trees
The amniote family tree put the amniote root at 319 Myr. The eukaryote class tree is a completely different file, built from a different set of studies at a different taxonomic rank, but it contains Mammalia and Aves as tips. Write a divergence(root, a, b) helper and use it to date the mammal–bird split in this tree, then compare.








Hint 1: The split date of two tips is the age of their most recent common ancestor, and you already have a helper on this page that finds that node from two names. What it stores is measured from the root, and you want years before today.


Hint 2: In a dated tree, every living tip is the same total distance from the root, so the root’s age is the depth of any tip (safest: the maximum leaf depth). Age and depth run in opposite directions.

Question. The two printed dates should agree exactly. Consider, why is the mammal–bird split the very same event as the amniote root?


2 · Design the visualisation
Before drawing, look at how other designers handle a tree too large for a conventional diagram:

explore the zoomable, fractal layout in the OneZoom Tree of Life Explorer;
inspect how evidence and taxonomic uncertainty are shown in the Open Tree of Life browser;
compare rectangular, circular, radial and annotated examples in the Interactive Tree of Life gallery.

For each site, note what one tip represents, whether branch length encodes time, how labels appear as the tree grows, what colour encodes, and what interaction contributes. Identify one design decision that helps the reader and one that would not work in a static course figure.
Now make your own eukaryote tree of life using either eukaryotes_class.nwk (235 class-level tips) or eukaryotes_phylum.nwk (66 phylum-level tips). Adapt the rectangular, circular or time-axis code from this page, or devise another node-link layout. Do not call the result a tree of all life: these files contain Eukaryota, not Bacteria or Archaea.

make the root, tips and branching structure readable;
state what one tip represents and report the number of tips;
say whether branch length means time and give its unit;
use colour only for named clades or another stated variable;
explain any labels, taxa or detail you omit;
include a title, caption, TimeTree source, and a note naming one online design that influenced your choices.







